  "preamble": "Raising of the open-mid back unrounded [ʌ] to the close central [ɨ]. In Korean, the Late Middle Korean vowel ㆍ (arae-a, Yale o, given as [ʌ]) merged with ㅡ (Yale u, [ɨ]) in syllables after the first, while in the first syllable of a word it merged with a; only Jeju keeps a distinct reflex. Middle Korean 오ᄂᆞᆯ 'today' thus became 오늘 oneul and 다ᄅᆞ다 'to differ' became 다르다 dareuda.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Raising, Centralization, Merger",
